+- **When to use**: When a hexagonal cross-section is needed. Use Variant A for speed and simplicity if the orientation matches. Use Variant B for strict geometric accuracy.
 
 ## Background
-The Hexagonal Prism SDF is a standard primitive in the family of Signed Distance Functions for ray marching. It was popularized by Inigo Quilez in his online articles and code repositories on SDFs [S2, unofficial]. The implementation in [S1, unofficial] is a PyTorch adaptation of similar GLSL primitives. The mathematical derivation relies on the symmetry of the hexagon to reduce the distance calculation to a simple formula in the fundamental domain.
+Signed Distance Functions for primitives are widely used in ray marching and implicit modeling. The hexagonal prism SDF is a standard primitive in libraries like [glsl-sdf-primitives](https://github.com/marklundin/glsl-sdf-primitives) [S2, unofficial] and [Torch-Sphere-Tracer](https://github.com/skmhrk1209/Torch-Sphere-Tracer) [S1, unofficial].
 
